{% doc %}
  blocks/subheading.liquid

  An eyebrow — the short line that sits above a heading — optionally with an
  icon beside it.

  ## Why this is a file and not an eleventh preset on the Text block

  `blocks/text.liquid` already has a Subheading preset, and it stays: a run of
  text set in the small-caps treatment is a *typography* choice, and that is
  what a preset is for. This block is a different thing standing next to it —
  a row. It holds an icon and a line of words on one line, with a gap between
  them, and a preset cannot introduce a second child element.

  Everything that is *not* the row is the Text block's, unchanged and by
  reference rather than by copy:

    typography, colour, presets   `snippets/block-typography.liquid`, and the
                                  `.block-text--*` classes in `assets/base.css`
    alignment, width              the same `.block-text--align-*` classes
    background panel              `.block-text--has-background`, driven by the
                                  same `--block-text-background` / `-radius`
    highlight                     the same `.block-text--highlight-*` classes
    padding                       `snippets/spacing.liquid`
    entrance animation            `snippets/motion-attrs.liquid`

  So this file's root element carries `.block-text` as well as its own class,
  and a change to Theme settings › Typography moves the Subheading block with
  every other run of text on the page. There is no second copy of the type map
  here, and nothing to keep in step by hand.

  ## The icon has two sources and a switch, not three fields

  `blocks/badge-highlight.liquid` tries three sources in order — pasted SVG,
  then an upload, then the theme set — and the merchant discovers which one won
  by clearing fields until the right icon appears. Here the source is stated:
  **None**, **Icon** or **Image**, and only the field belonging to the chosen
  source is shown. There is no precedence to learn because there is no contest.

  **Gap** follows the same rule. With no icon there is nothing for a gap to sit
  between, so the control is hidden rather than left in the panel doing nothing.

  ## `block_level: true` on the animation

  The splitting presets — words, letters — take an element's text apart and
  animate the pieces. On a pill with an icon in it that reads as the label
  coming apart while the icon sits still. `block_level` resolves the merchant's
  cascade exactly as usual and then maps any splitting preset onto the
  whole-element preset that travels the same way, which is what the parameter
  exists for.

  ## One style attribute

  Spacing and typography are pulled in as declarations rather than as their own
  attributes. Two `style` attributes on one element is invalid HTML, the browser
  keeps the first and discards the rest, and that is how padding sliders end up
  moving a value that never reaches the page.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  assign preset = s.preset | default: 'subheading'
  assign alignment = s.alignment | default: 'start'
  assign alignment_mobile = s.alignment_mobile | default: 'start'
  assign color_mode = s.color | default: 'inherit'
  assign highlight = s.highlight_style | default: 'none'
  assign background_mode = s.background | default: 'inherit'
  assign icon_source = s.icon_source | default: 'none'

  # ---- tag ----------------------------------------------------------------
  # Identical to blocks/text.liquid, deliberately. The preset select offers the
  # same ten options, so it has to mean the same ten things: `Default` leaves
  # the merchant's markup alone, and any heading preset normalises the tags to
  # that level so the size and the document outline agree.
  assign heading_levels = 'h1 h2 h3 h4 h5 h6'
  assign is_heading = false
  if heading_levels contains preset
    assign is_heading = true
  endif

  assign body = s.text

  if is_heading
    assign open_tag = '<' | append: preset | append: '>'
    assign close_tag = '</' | append: preset | append: '>'
    assign body = body | replace: '<p>', open_tag | replace: '</p>', close_tag

    assign levels = heading_levels | split: ' '
    for level in levels
      unless level == preset
        assign find_open = '<' | append: level | append: '>'
        assign find_close = '</' | append: level | append: '>'
        assign body = body | replace: find_open, open_tag | replace: find_close, close_tag
      endunless
    endfor
  endif

  # ---- icon ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # The source decides, and the field belonging to it has to actually hold
  # something: a merchant who picks Image and has not uploaded one yet gets the
  # text on its own rather than an empty box holding a gap open.
  assign has_icon = false
  if icon_source == 'icon' and s.icon != blank and s.icon != 'none'
    assign has_icon = true
  elsif icon_source == 'image' and s.icon_image != blank
    assign has_icon = true
  endif

  assign icon_size = s.icon_size | default: 20 | plus: 0

  comment
    `icon_style` is the *box* around the glyph, and it is the one control that
    decides whether there is a box at all:

      none     no box. The glyph or image sits straight beside the words at
               Icon size, and width, height, border and radius are hidden
               because there is nothing for them to apply to.
      solid    a filled box, painted from the scheme's primary button colours
      outline  a transparent box carrying the border below

    Written this way there is no combination that does nothing. The alternative
    — a box that always exists, plus a separate "show background" toggle — has
    two settings able to contradict each other, which is the state the header
    notes on `sections/header.liquid` describe as the one worth designing out.

    The solid colours are derived rather than exposed as two more swatches, the
    same way `snippets/color-schemes.liquid` derives the cart bubble and the
    badges from the scheme it is already in: a merchant who switches scheme gets
    an icon that still belongs to it, and nobody has to remember a second pair
    of colours per scheme.
  endcomment
  assign icon_style = s.icon_style | default: 'none'

  assign icon_box_width = s.icon_width | default: 40 | plus: 0
  assign icon_box_height = s.icon_height | default: 40 | plus: 0
  assign icon_radius = s.icon_border_radius | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign icon_border_style = s.icon_border_style | default: 'none'
  assign icon_border_width = s.icon_border_thickness | default: 0 | plus: 0

  comment
    The image is sized by the same Icon size the glyph uses — one number for
    both sources, so switching between them does not resize the icon — and it
    keeps its own aspect ratio. `ratio: 'square'` used to be passed here, which
    forced a 1:1 box and cropped anything that was not square; omitting it lets
    `snippets/image.liquid` fall through to `adapt`, which reserves the upload's
    real proportions.

    `sizes` follows the same number rather than a hardcoded `48px`. A hint that
    says 48 while the merchant has asked for 20 is asking the browser for a file
    more than twice the width it will paint.
  endcomment
  assign icon_image_sizes = icon_size | append: 'px'

  comment
    The divider is a rule *between* the icon and the words, so it cannot exist
    without both. Tied to `has_icon` rather than to the Show divider checkbox
    alone: a merchant who turns the icon off and leaves the checkbox on would
    otherwise get a stray vertical line at the start of the eyebrow with nothing
    on the other side of it.

    It is an empty element rather than a border on the icon box or a `::before`
    on the text. A border on the box cannot be inset from the box's own edges,
    which is the whole look — the rule is shorter than the row and centred on
    it. A pseudo-element on the text would have to be positioned out of flow to
    sit in the gap, and would then ignore the Gap slider entirely; as a flex
    child it simply takes the gap on both sides, for free.
  endcomment
  assign has_divider = false
  if has_icon and s.show_divider
    assign has_divider = true
  endif

  # ---- the block's own border ---------------------------------------------
  # Independent of the background: an outlined pill with nothing behind it is a
  # shape merchants ask for constantly, and gating the outline on a background
  # would make it unreachable. Radius is independent of both, which is why it
  # is no longer hidden when the background is set to Inherit.
  assign border_style = s.border_style | default: 'none'
  assign border_width = s.border_thickness | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign has_border = false
  if border_style != 'none' and border_width > 0
    assign has_border = true
  endif

  # ---- width --------------------------------------------------------------
  # Both a `flex` and an `inline-size`, for the reason `blocks/text.liquid`
  # records: this block is only a flex *item* when its parent is a group, so at
  # the top level of a section a `flex` declaration is inert and Fit would do
  # nothing on its own.
  #
  # Fit is the default here where Fill is the default on the Text block. A
  # subheading with a background is a pill, and a pill that spans the container
  # is a band — so the box hugs its contents and the alignment classes move it,
  # which is the shape the design asks for without a single new rule.
  if s.width == 'fill'
    assign width_flex = '1 1 0%'
    assign width_size = '100%'
  else
    assign width_flex = '0 0 auto'
    assign width_size = 'fit-content'
  endif

  if s.width_mobile == 'fill'
    assign width_flex_mobile = '1 1 0%'
    assign width_size_mobile = '100%'
  else
    assign width_flex_mobile = '0 0 auto'
    assign width_size_mobile = 'fit-content'
  endif

  # ---- the row's own alignment --------------------------------------------
  # `.block-text--align-*` moves the *box* — `text-align`, `align-self` and an
  # auto margin. None of those position flex children, so a block set to Fill is
  # a full-width row with its icon and words stuck at the start whatever the
  # alignment says.
  #
  # Emitted as custom properties rather than three more classes, which is what
  # `snippets/alignment.liquid` does for the same reason: the setting's three
  # values map one-for-one onto flex keywords, so a lookup table in CSS would be
  # a second copy of a map that already exists here.
  case alignment
    when 'center'
      assign justify = 'center'
    when 'end'
      assign justify = 'flex-end'
    else
      assign justify = 'flex-start'
  endcase

  case alignment_mobile
    when 'center'
      assign justify_mobile = 'center'
    when 'end'
      assign justify_mobile = 'flex-end'
    else
      assign justify_mobile = 'flex-start'
  endcase

  # ---- classes ------------------------------------------------------------
  # `.block-text` and `.rte` sit on this element alongside `.block-subheading`,
  # which is what hands the whole Text block stylesheet to this block. The
  # preset, colour and highlight rules in `assets/base.css` all select on
  # `.block-text…`, and several of them need `.rte` on the same element.
  assign classes = 'block-subheading block-text rte'
  assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--' | append: preset
  assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--align-' | append: alignment
  assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--align-mobile-' | append: alignment_mobile

  comment
    The Text block's two wrapping controls — Measure and "Keep on one line on
    mobile" — are deliberately not offered here. Both govern where a paragraph
    breaks, and an eyebrow is one short line: a measure it never reaches, and a
    wrap it never makes. Leaving them in would be two settings a merchant can
    move all day with nothing happening on the page, which is the failure this
    theme's own notes keep coming back to.
  endcomment

  if color_mode == 'gradient'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--gradient'
  elsif color_mode != 'inherit' and color_mode != blank
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--colored'
  endif

  if highlight != 'none' and highlight != blank
    assign highlight_target = s.highlight_target | default: 'em'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--highlight block-text--highlight-' | append: highlight
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--hl-' | append: highlight_target
  endif

  comment
    `inherit` emits no background at all, so whatever colour scheme the section
    is using shows through — the same contract the Text block's colour select
    keeps, and the reason neither needs a "transparent" swatch. Only `custom`
    turns the panel on, and with it `.block-text--has-background` brings the
    padding that keeps a background off the words.
  endcomment
  if background_mode == 'custom'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-text--has-background'
  endif

  comment
    A border needs the same padding a background needs, and for the same reason
    the Text block's note gives: a rule drawn flush against its own words reads
    as a mistake, and making the merchant go and find the spacing sliders to fix
    it makes the control feel broken. The class only sets `--block-padding-*`
    defaults, so a slider that has left zero still wins.
  endcomment
  if has_border
    assign classes = classes | append: ' block-subheading--bordered'
  endif

  comment
    No `--has-icon` class. The icon element either exists or it does not, and the
    gap is only emitted when it does — a class saying the same thing a third
    time is a fact free to disagree with the other two. `snippets/button.liquid`
    records what that costs: `button--fx-swap` was dropped and every rule still
    naming it went dead in silence.

    The icon's *surface* does get one, because solid and outline are two
    discrete treatments rather than a number — the same split the theme keeps
    everywhere: presets are classes, measurements are custom properties.
  endcomment
  assign icon_classes = 'block-subheading__icon block-subheading__icon--' | append: icon_style
-%}

{%- capture block_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  {%- render 'block-typography', block: s -%}
  --block-text-flex: {{ width_flex }};
  --block-text-width: {{ width_size }};
  --block-text-flex-mobile: {{ width_flex_mobile }};
  --block-text-width-mobile: {{ width_size_mobile }};
  --subheading-justify: {{ justify }};
  --subheading-justify-mobile: {{ justify_mobile }};
  {%- if has_icon -%}
    --subheading-icon-size: {{ icon_size }}px;
    --subheading-gap: {{ s.gap | default: 8 | plus: 0 }}px;
    {%- comment -%}
      Nothing about the box is emitted for the `none` style, so the fallbacks in
      `assets/base.css` — `auto` for both axes, `0` for the radius and the
      border — leave the glyph sitting on its own with no box to size.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Every colour below is emitted only when the merchant actually picked one.
      Left blank, the property is never written and the fallback in
      `assets/base.css` resolves — which is the scheme's own colour, not a
      hardcoded hex. That is what keeps an icon belonging to whatever scheme the
      section is set to until somebody deliberately overrides it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if s.icon_color != blank -%}
      --subheading-icon-color: {{ s.icon_color }};
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if icon_style != 'none' -%}
      --subheading-icon-box-width: {{ icon_box_width }}px;
      --subheading-icon-box-height: {{ icon_box_height }}px;
      --subheading-icon-radius: {{ icon_radius }}px;
      {%- if icon_style == 'solid' and s.icon_background != blank -%}
        --subheading-icon-background: {{ s.icon_background }};
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if icon_border_style != 'none' and icon_border_width > 0 -%}
        --subheading-icon-border-width: {{ icon_border_width }}px;
        --subheading-icon-border-style: {{ icon_border_style }};
        {%- if s.icon_border_color != blank -%}
          --subheading-icon-border-color: {{ s.icon_border_color }};
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if has_divider and s.divider_color != blank -%}
      --subheading-divider-color: {{ s.divider_color }};
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if highlight != 'none' and s.highlight_color != blank -%}
    --block-highlight-color: {{ s.highlight_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The radius is emitted whatever the background is set to. It used to sit
    inside the `custom` branch, which tied it to a setting it has nothing to do
    with: a merchant outlining a transparent pill got a radius control that
    silently did nothing until they also turned a background on.

    It keeps the name `--block-text-radius` rather than gaining a
    `--subheading-` twin, because `.block-text--has-background` already reads
    that property. Two names for one number is how the two rounded corners end
    up disagreeing.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --block-text-radius: {{ s.border_radius | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- if background_mode == 'custom' and s.background_color != blank -%}
    --block-text-background: {{ s.background_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if has_border -%}
    --subheading-border-width: {{ border_width }}px;
    --subheading-border-style: {{ border_style }};
    {%- if s.border_color != blank -%}
      --subheading-border-color: {{ s.border_color }};
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- if s.text != blank -%}
  <motion-effect
    {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'text', block: s, block_level: true %}
    class="{{ classes }}"
    data-block-spacing
    style="{{ block_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
    {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
  >
    {%- if has_icon -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        No `aria-hidden` on this box, and that is a change rather than an
        omission.

        It used to be here on the grounds that an eyebrow announcing "star, new
        arrivals" reads the ornament aloud before the thing it decorates — true
        of the glyph, and `snippets/icon.liquid` already marks every glyph it
        renders `aria-hidden`, so that case was covered twice.

        It is not true of an upload. An **Alt text** field now sits beside the
        image picker, and `aria-hidden` on the wrapper would suppress whatever
        the merchant typed into it — a field that looks like it works and is
        silently discarded before it reaches anyone. Left off, each source
        carries its own semantics: the glyph hides itself, and an image is
        decorative exactly when its alt is empty, which is the rule the platform
        already has.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span class="{{ icon_classes }}">
        {%- if icon_source == 'image' -%}
          {% render 'image',
             image: s.icon_image,
             alt: s.icon_image_alt,
             sizes: icon_image_sizes,
             wrapper_class: 'block-subheading__image' %}
        {%- else -%}
          {% render 'icon', icon: s.icon, size: icon_size %}
        {%- endif -%}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if has_divider -%}
      <span class="block-subheading__divider" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="block-subheading__text">{{ body }}</div>
  </motion-effect>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.subheading.name",
  "tag": null,
  "settings": [
    { "type": "richtext", "id": "text", "label": "t:shared.text.label", "default": "<p>Subheading</p>" },

    { "type": "header", "content": "t:blocks.subheading.headers.icon" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "t:blocks.subheading.paragraph" },
    {
      "type": "select", "id": "icon_source", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "none", "label": "t:shared.none.label" },
        { "value": "icon", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "image", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.options__3.label" }
      ],
      "default": "icon"
    },
    {
      "type": "select", "id": "icon", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "star", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "check", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "heart", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__3.label" },
        { "value": "tag", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__4.label" },
        { "value": "truck", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__5.label" },
        { "value": "refresh", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__6.label" },
        { "value": "lock", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__7.label" },
        { "value": "clock", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__8.label" },
        { "value": "location", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__9.label" },
        { "value": "mail", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__10.label" },
        { "value": "note", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__11.label" },
        { "value": "info", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__12.label" }
      ],
      "default": "star",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'icon' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker", "id": "icon_image", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image.label", "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'image' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "text", "id": "icon_image_alt", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image_alt.label", "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image_alt.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'image' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range", "id": "icon_size", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_size.label", "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_size.info",
      "min": 12, "max": 48, "step": 2, "unit": "px", "default": 20,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "select", "id": "icon_style", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_style.label", "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_style.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "none", "label": "t:shared.none.label" },
        { "value": "solid", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_style.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "outline", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_style.options__3.label" }
      ],
      "default": "solid",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color", "id": "icon_background", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_background.label", "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_background.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source != 'none' and block.settings.icon_style == 'solid' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color", "id": "icon_color", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_color.label", "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_color.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'icon' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range", "id": "icon_width", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_width.label",
      "min": 16, "max": 96, "step": 2, "unit": "px", "default": 40,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source != 'none' and block.settings.icon_style != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range", "id": "icon_height", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_height.label",
      "min": 16, "max": 96, "step": 2, "unit": "px", "default": 40,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source != 'none' and block.settings.icon_style != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "select", "id": "icon_border_style", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_border_style.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "none", "label": "t:shared.none.label" },
        { "value": "solid", "label": "t:shared.border.style.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "dashed", "label": "t:shared.border.style.options__2.label" }
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source != 'none' and block.settings.icon_style != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range", "id": "icon_border_thickness", "label": "t:shared.border.thickness.label",
      "min": 0, "max": 8, "step": 1, "unit": "px", "default": 1,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source != 'none' and block.settings.icon_style != 'none' and block.settings.icon_border_style != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color", "id": "icon_border_color", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_border_color.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source != 'none' and block.settings.icon_style != 'none' and block.settings.icon_border_style != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range", "id": "icon_border_radius", "label": "t:shared.border.radius.label", "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_border_radius.info",
      "min": 0, "max": 50, "step": 2, "unit": "px", "default": 50,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source != 'none' and block.settings.icon_style != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_divider", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.show_divider.label", "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.show_divider.info",
      "default": true,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color", "id": "divider_color", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.divider_color.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source != 'none' and block.settings.show_divider }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range", "id": "gap", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.gap.label", "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.gap.info",
      "min": 0, "max": 24, "step": 2, "unit": "px", "default": 8,
